        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "All other constraints are first class and no further constraints need to be imposed for consistency. There are consequently five first class constraints ( $ \\Phi^U $,  $ \\Phi_k^A $ and  $ \\Sigma $) and six second class constraints ( $ \\Sigma_i $ and  $ T_k $). The constraints  $ \\Phi^U $ and  $ \\Sigma $ correspond to the usual gauge transformations  $ \\delta W_0 = \\partial_0 \\Omega $,  $ \\delta W_i = \\partial_i \\Omega $ associated with a gauge field  $ W_\\mu $, while  $ \\Phi_k^A $ is associated with the fact that in (12)  $ A_k $ acts merely as a Lagrange multiplier (i.e., it is not dynamical) and hence its value is completely arbitrary. Suitable gauge conditions associated with the first class constraints are",
